  {
   "id": "yaNOpWQXDwAJ",
   "from": "Andrew Willis",
   "author": "38ec465044fe43d2",
   "date": "2017-03-15T03:58:17Z",
   "subject": "Re: self joins for year-over-year analysis",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Thank you Brandon for your thoughts. <div><br></div><div>I&#39;m not able to use a lag to get the behavior I want because the day that I want to compare to can vary. For example, I want to compare the first Monday in March of 2017 with the first Monday in March of 2016. There are a few more details into how we figure out which days to compare, so we have a database table that has a row for each day, along with the corresponding day in the prior year.</div><div><br></div><div>While the data I have right now is in a single table (it&#39;s daily sales figures), I could definitely filter it out into two separate tibbles and then join them. It sounds like that extra step of dividing the data into two sets is going to be needed. </div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>Thanks again,</div><div>-Andrew<br></div><div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>On Tuesday, March 14, 2017 at 10:57:16 AM UTC-4, Brandon Hurr wrote:</div></div>"
    ],
